\begin_layout Itemize
Density operators have special properties.
 It can be shown that:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
they have unit trace:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Tr}\,\hat{\rho}=c_{0}c_{0}^{\ast}+c_{1}c_{1}^{\ast}=1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
they are Hermitian:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\rho}=\hat{\rho}^{\dagger}
\]

\end_inset

or,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{ij}=\rho_{ji}^{\ast}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can expand any single-qubit density operator over the matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\mathbf{1},\hat{\sigma}_{x},\hat{\sigma}_{y},\hat{\sigma}_{z}\}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\rho}=\frac{1}{2}\left(a_{0}\mathbf{1}+a_{x}\hat{\sigma}_{x}+a_{y}\hat{\sigma}_{y}+a_{z}\hat{\sigma}_{z}\right)\label{eq:DensityOoperatorHermitianExpansion}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with real coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{0},a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For a simpler notation,
 we will define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{0}=\mathbf{1},\quad\hat{\sigma}_{1}=\hat{\sigma}_{x},\quad\hat{\sigma}_{2}=\hat{\sigma}_{y},\quad\mbox{and}\quad\hat{\sigma}_{3}=\hat{\sigma}_{z},\label{eq:sigma_matrices}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and we rewrite our expansion as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\rho}=\sum_{k=0}^{3}\frac{1}{2}a_{k}\hat{\sigma}_{k}\label{eq:DensityOperatorHermitianExpansionConvenient}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can we can calculate the expansion coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{k}=\mbox{Tr}\;\left(\hat{\rho}\hat{\sigma}_{k}\right)\stackrel{\left(\ref{eq:ExpectationValueDensityMatrix}\right)}{=}\Braket{\hat{\sigma}_{k}}\label{eq:CoherenceVectorComponents}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For any normalized 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}=1$
\end_inset

,
 so this doesn't tell us much information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
On the other hand,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3})$
\end_inset

 are more informative
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
They will all have a value in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $-1\leq a_{k}\leq1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can visualize the quantum state in 3D if we define the 
\series bold
Bloch vector
\series default
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\lambda}$
\end_inset

,
 as a vector in real space (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To form a Bloch vector,
 we discard 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}$
\end_inset

 and make a 3D vector using 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{3}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 in this example,
 our Bloch vector is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{\lambda}\equiv\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}\\
a_{2}\\
a_{3}
\end{bmatrix}.\label{eq:BlochVector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The Bloch vector components also may be calculated in state vector formalism as
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}=\braket{\hat{\sigma}_{k}}\stackrel{\left(\ref{eq:ExpectationValueStateVector}\right)}{=}\braket{\psi|\hat{\sigma}_{k}|\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{k}=\braket{\hat{\sigma}_{k}}=\braket{\psi|\hat{\sigma}_{k}|\psi}\label{eq:BlochVectComponentsExpectationValueStateVect}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

